<section
  id="methodology"
  class="methodology"
  aria-labelledby="methodology-heading"
>
  <header class="methodology-header">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label">Methodology & limitations</p>
      <h2 id="methodology-heading">
        A transparent thought tool, not a prediction engine
      </h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      The model deliberately exposes its assumptions. Its weights are normative
      design choices—not empirically validated coefficients, probabilities, or a
      social index.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="formula-column">
      <h3>The scoring sequence</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <span>Institutional capacity</span>
          <code>
            0.21(ownership) + 0.18(democracy) + 0.16(social dividend) +
            0.13(worker power) + 0.13(civil liberties) + 0.11(universal access)
            + 0.08(open infrastructure)
          </code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Automation amplifier</span>
          <code>0.55 + 0.90 × (automation / 100)</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Internal outcome position</span>
          <code
            >clamp(50 + (institutional capacity − 50) × amplifier, 0, 100)</code
          >
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="formula-note">
        The internal position selects a scenario band, but the number itself is
        not shown as a probability or validated measure. Low automation
        compresses the possible range toward the middle. High automation makes
        both strong and weak institutions more consequential.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="limits-column">
      <h3>What the model leaves out</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Feedback loops between technology, wealth, law, culture, conflict, and
          public opinion.
        </li>
        <li>
          International differences, ecological constraints, demographic change,
          and transition speed.
        </li>
        <li>
          How institutions work in practice rather than how they are described
          formally.
        </li>
        <li>
          Interactions, thresholds, and path dependence beyond the single
          explicit tail-risk guard.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The purpose is to make assumptions visible and discussable. Visitors
        should challenge the weights, categories, and missing variables rather
        than treating them as settled science.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guard-note">
    <span aria-hidden="true">!</span>
    <div>
      <h3>Why the darkest scenario has an additional guard</h3>
      <p>
        “Eliminationist Regime” appears in the live model only when near-total
        automation coincides with extremely concentrated ownership,
        authoritarian rule, destroyed civil liberties, negligible bargaining
        power, minimal social claims, and a result below the lowest threshold.
        Genocide also requires political ideology, dehumanization, institutions,
        perpetrators, and the destruction of restraints. AI does not
        automatically produce it. The concern is narrower: extreme automation
        and surveillance could remove some practical barriers to mass coercion
        if every relevant democratic and moral protection failed as well.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <details class="indicator-formulas">
    <summary>See the five structural indicator formulas</summary>
    <div>
      <p>
        <code>Material abundance = 0.65 automation + 0.35 universal access</code
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <code
          >Shared prosperity = 0.40 ownership + 0.25 social dividend + 0.20
          worker power + 0.15 universal access</code
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <code
          >Personal autonomy = 0.35 democracy + 0.30 civil liberties + 0.20
          worker power + 0.15 universal access</code
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <code
          >Political security = 0.55 democracy + 0.30 civil liberties + 0.15
          ownership</code
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <code
          >Freedom from compulsory work = automation × [0.30 + 0.70 ×
          ((ownership + social dividend + universal access) / 300)]</code
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        Every indicator is clamped to 0–100. These are conceptual composites for
        the interface, not measurements or forecasts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

